1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,519
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,519 --> 00:00:05,452
In this lesson you'll learn what to do when your dataset has missing values.

3
00:00:05,452 --> 00:00:06,944
First comes the theory:

4
00:00:06,944 --> 00:00:09,281
the three reasons data goes missing.

5
00:00:09,281 --> 00:00:15,381
Then a hands-on lab in Python, using nearly fifty thousand real New York City Airbnb listings.

6
00:00:15,381 --> 00:00:24,792
By the end, you'll be able to detect missing values, work out why they're missing, fill them in four different ways, and judge which fix is best.

7
00:00:25,492 --> 00:00:27,620
No real dataset arrives complete.

8
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:32,520
Forms get skipped, sensors drop out, and some values simply don't exist yet.

9
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:36,453
If you drop every incomplete row, you throw away information.

10
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:41,547
If you fill blindly, you can distort the very statistics you wanted to compute.

11
00:00:41,547 --> 00:00:47,607
In our lab data, twenty point six percent of listings are missing the reviews per month value.

12
00:00:47,607 --> 00:00:49,413
That's far too many to drop.

13
00:00:49,413 --> 00:00:52,056
So the right fix depends on one question:

14
00:00:52,056 --> 00:00:53,668
why is the value missing?

15
00:00:54,368 --> 00:00:57,487
Every missing value fits one of three patterns.

16
00:00:57,487 --> 00:01:03,392
M C A R, missing completely at random, means the gap has no relationship to any variable.

17
00:01:03,392 --> 00:01:06,444
It's pure chance, like a random sensor glitch.

18
00:01:06,444 --> 00:01:11,885
M A R, missing at random, means the gap depends on another column you can observe.

19
00:01:11,885 --> 00:01:16,132
For example, retired people skip the salary question more often.

20
00:01:16,132 --> 00:01:25,222
And M N A R, missing not at random, means the gap depends on the hidden value itself, like high earners declining to report their income.

21
00:01:25,222 --> 00:01:30,464
The pattern decides whether an imputation method helps or quietly misleads you.

22
00:01:31,164 --> 00:01:33,087
Let's see this in our data.

23
00:01:33,087 --> 00:01:36,578
Reviews per month is missing for 10,116 listings.

24
00:01:36,578 --> 00:01:41,493
We checked, and every single one of those rows also has zero reviews.

25
00:01:41,493 --> 00:01:43,701
That's the definition of M A R.

26
00:01:43,701 --> 00:01:52,605
A listing with no reviews has no reviews per month rate to record, so the gap is fully explained by a column we already have.

27
00:01:52,605 --> 00:02:00,370
By contrast, the name and host name columns have only sixteen and twenty-one gaps, scattered with no pattern.

28
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:03,718
That's consistent with M C A R, but be careful:

29
00:02:03,718 --> 00:02:06,852
M C A R can never be proven from data alone.

30
00:02:07,552 --> 00:02:08,537
Now the lab.

31
00:02:08,537 --> 00:02:12,148
We load the cleaned Airbnb file with pandas.

32
00:02:12,148 --> 00:02:14,939
It has 49,195 rows and 16 columns.

33
00:02:14,939 --> 00:02:20,192
Calling isnull dot sum gives the missing count for every column.

34
00:02:20,192 --> 00:02:22,490
Only four columns have gaps:

35
00:02:22,490 --> 00:02:26,758
name, host name, last review, and reviews per month.

36
00:02:26,758 --> 00:02:29,056
Everything else is complete.

37
00:02:29,756 --> 00:02:31,273
Counting gaps is easy.

38
00:02:31,273 --> 00:02:33,549
Explaining them is the real work.

39
00:02:33,549 --> 00:02:35,894
This crosstab compares two things:

40
00:02:35,894 --> 00:02:40,307
is reviews per month missing, and is the number of reviews zero.

41
00:02:40,307 --> 00:02:41,341
Read the table.

42
00:02:41,341 --> 00:02:45,755
When the value is missing, the number of reviews is always zero.

43
00:02:45,755 --> 00:02:48,100
And the other two cells are empty.

44
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:50,996
That turns our guess into a verified fact.

45
00:02:51,696 --> 00:02:52,761
One more trap:

46
00:02:52,761 --> 00:02:54,891
gaps that isnull cannot see.

47
00:02:54,891 --> 00:02:58,618
A price of zero, found in 11 rows, is impossible.

48
00:02:58,618 --> 00:03:05,159
A minimum stay of nine hundred ninety-nine nights, in 3 rows, is an old sentinel code.

49
00:03:05,159 --> 00:03:12,080
And 8 host names contain the literal text Nan or Na, which pandas reads as a normal string.

50
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:15,351
Convert these to real missing values first.

51
00:03:15,351 --> 00:03:16,644
But use judgment.

52
00:03:16,644 --> 00:03:21,816
Zero reviews and zero available days are real data, not hidden gaps.

53
00:03:22,516 --> 00:03:24,049
Now to fixing the gaps.

54
00:03:24,049 --> 00:03:33,378
Imputation replaces a missing value with an estimate, and every method trades simplicity for how much of the data's real structure it keeps.

55
00:03:33,378 --> 00:03:39,643
Mean or median is the quick baseline, but every fill is identical, so it shrinks the variance.

56
00:03:39,643 --> 00:03:44,641
K-N-N borrows from similar rows, but it's slower and needs scaled features.

57
00:03:44,641 --> 00:03:49,972
Regression predicts the value from other columns, but it can extrapolate poorly.

58
00:03:50,672 --> 00:03:51,472
Method one:

59
00:03:51,472 --> 00:03:52,636
mean and median.

60
00:03:52,636 --> 00:04:00,419
We compute the fill value from the observed rows only, then fillna applies it to every missing row at once.

61
00:04:00,419 --> 00:04:02,601
The observed variance is 2.82.

62
00:04:02,601 --> 00:04:07,766
Mean-fill keeps 79.4 percent of it, and median-fill keeps 82.0 percent.

63
00:04:07,766 --> 00:04:14,312
Because this column is strongly right-skewed, the median is the more honest single number.

64
00:04:15,012 --> 00:04:15,837
Method two:

65
00:04:15,837 --> 00:04:17,112
K-N-N imputation.

66
00:04:17,112 --> 00:04:28,809
For each missing row, the imputer finds the five most similar listings by price, location, availability, and the other features, then averages their values.

67
00:04:28,809 --> 00:04:33,308
It keeps 86.7 percent of the variance, the best of the four.

68
00:04:33,308 --> 00:04:41,107
And for the originally missing rows it filled an average of 0.41, far below the blind mean-fill of 1.37.

69
00:04:41,107 --> 00:04:46,356
K-N-N was never told about the zero reviews rule, yet it respected it.

70
00:04:47,056 --> 00:04:48,043
Method three:

71
00:04:48,043 --> 00:04:48,878
regression.

72
00:04:48,878 --> 00:05:00,266
We train a linear model on the rows where the value is known, then predict the missing ones, clipping at zero because a review rate can't be negative.

73
00:05:00,266 --> 00:05:03,379
The R squared is only 0.33, a modest fit.

74
00:05:03,379 --> 00:05:09,377
The model predicts 0.78 for the zero-review rows, too high compared with K-N-N.

75
00:05:09,377 --> 00:05:14,236
Variance retained is 81.9 percent, about the same as the median.

76
00:05:14,236 --> 00:05:18,184
A weak R squared is a warning sign, not a free pass.

77
00:05:18,884 --> 00:05:21,892
Here's what each fill does to the distribution.

78
00:05:21,892 --> 00:05:23,876
On the left, the observed data.

79
00:05:23,876 --> 00:05:29,188
In the middle, mean-filling stacks more than ten thousand rows on one single value.

80
00:05:29,188 --> 00:05:31,940
On the right, K-N-N keeps a natural spread.

81
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:34,436
That's what losing variance looks like.

82
00:05:35,136 --> 00:05:39,345
Lined up side by side, the trade-offs become numbers.

83
00:05:39,345 --> 00:05:45,062
Mean retains 79.4 percent, regression 81.9, median 82.0, and K-N-N 86.7.

84
00:05:45,062 --> 00:05:52,209
Every method loses some spread, because filling in a guess can never add real information.

85
00:05:52,209 --> 00:05:55,067
The lesson isn't never use the mean.

86
00:05:55,067 --> 00:05:59,832
It's that checking the variance change is part of the audit.

87
00:06:00,532 --> 00:06:01,489
Let's recap.

88
00:06:01,489 --> 00:06:01,808
One:

89
00:06:01,808 --> 00:06:06,993
the mechanism, M C A R, M A R, or M N A R, should drive your fix.

90
00:06:06,993 --> 00:06:07,312
Two:

91
00:06:07,312 --> 00:06:11,140
prove M A R with a crosstab instead of guessing.

92
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:11,619
Three:

93
00:06:11,619 --> 00:06:14,969
hunt for disguised gaps before you impute.

94
00:06:14,969 --> 00:06:15,367
Four:

95
00:06:15,367 --> 00:06:21,988
mean and median are simplest but lose the most variance, while K-N-N kept the most.

96
00:06:21,988 --> 00:06:22,705
And five:

97
00:06:22,705 --> 00:06:27,172
always compare strategies and check the variance change.

98
00:06:27,872 --> 00:06:29,115
Now try it yourself.

99
00:06:29,115 --> 00:06:38,249
Run the detection steps on a column in your own dataset, note which observed column explains the gaps, and compare at least two imputation methods.

100
00:06:38,249 --> 00:06:40,797
In the next lesson we'll tackle outliers.

101
00:06:40,797 --> 00:06:43,717
If this helped, subscribe so you don't miss it.

102
00:06:43,717 --> 00:06:44,960
Thanks for watching.

